# grammar: v0 (unfrozen; AST normative)
#
# nine-realms — the base world of the Norse example: the Nine Realms on the
# branches of Yggdrasil, the ages running down toward Ragnarok, and the seeress
# who narrates the fall from the far side of the world's rebirth.
#
# A wholly public-domain setting (the Norse myths — the Aesir, the Nine Realms,
# the doom of the gods) chosen to prove the engine is genuinely world-agnostic:
# not one token of any production world appears here. This pack exercises the
# gentle subset plus the chronology spine end to end — a totally-ordered era
# spine (>=3 eras) running toward the doom with a narration vantage past it,
# events with a before-fact and a deliberately UNORDERED pair, cyclic time
# (season and the great-year wheel), a place adjacency graph (9 realms, >=6
# edges), people and a thing with facts, states with since/until across two eras,
# a WORLD RULE WITH NEGATION that derives a certain-at-risk conclusion, a coined
# term, a cited lexical ban, an open fork, an open question under a hypothesis,
# and a settled hypothesis via a ratify BLOCK. Provenance is this world's OWN
# decision list (d-0001..) — a solo writer's ledger, per 01-syntax §3.
scope nine-realms

# --- the world's ledger: the decisions its citations rest on ------------------
# Declaring a decision is what lets `decided d-000n` carry authority rather than
# mint it (B1): a citation to an undeclared decision fails closed.
ratify d-0001
ratify d-0002
ratify d-0003
ratify d-0004
ratify d-0005
ratify d-0006
ratify d-0007
ratify d-0008
ratify d-0009   # the cyclic-time cycles
ratify d-0010   # the ordering rule over the era spine
ratify d-0011   # the ward-holds negation rule and its ground
ratify d-0012   # the coined term
ratify d-0013   # the lexical ban
ratify d-0014   # the seeress-foresaw ratify block

# --- world chronology: a totally-ordered era spine (>=3 eras) -----------------
# The ages run down toward the doom; WITHIN an era only `before` orders events.
era elder-age < the-binding-age < the-wolf-age < after-ragnarok   decided d-0001

# events carry era membership; a deliberately UNORDERED pair sits in elder-age
event the-world-shaped      during elder-age        decided d-0002
event the-wall-raised       during elder-age        decided d-0002
event the-binding-of-fenrir during the-binding-age  decided d-0003
event the-death-of-baldr    during the-binding-age  decided d-0003
event fimbulwinter-falls    during the-wolf-age     proposed
event the-last-battle       during the-wolf-age     decided d-0004

# a declared intra-era order:
before the-binding-of-fenrir -> the-death-of-baldr   decided d-0003
# ...and a DELIBERATELY UNORDERED pair: the-world-shaped and the-wall-raised
# share elder-age and no before-fact orders them, so happens-before(x, y) answers
# `unordered` — a first-class verdict, not a silent tie-break (03-time §1).

narration vantage after-ragnarok
# inert: the seeress sings the doom looking back from the reborn world; declaring
# an event in the vantage era is a type error (03-time §1.iii).

# --- cyclic time: the wheels a practice may seat its hour on (03-time §1.iv) ---
# The four seasons and the great-year — the eternal return of the Norse cosmos,
# fire and rebirth. Cyclic kinds with no epoch and no calendar: succ/pred/across/
# distance exist, but `<`, sort, and max are type errors (the wheel refuses rank,
# koan 8). The moon's own wheel is seated by the seidr practice that needs it.
cycle season(4) = winter | spring | summer | autumn   decided d-0009
cycle great-year(4) = gold | strife | doom | reborn    decided d-0009

# --- an ordering rule over the era/event spine (03-time §6, block B) -----------
# A floor on when the deepest era may first be shown, held as data the season
# checker reads rather than as code — a declaration, not a running constraint.
rule elder-age-held-back over order   decided d-0010
  : listener-learns(during elder-age _) requires act > 1

# --- places and the adjacency graph (9 realms, >=6 edges) ---------------------
place asgard       "Asgard"        decided d-0004
place vanaheim     "Vanaheim"      decided d-0004
place alfheim      "Alfheim"       decided d-0004
place midgard      "Midgard"       decided d-0005
place jotunheim    "Jotunheim"     decided d-0005
place svartalfheim "Svartalfheim"  decided d-0005
place muspelheim   "Muspelheim"    decided d-0006
place niflheim     "Niflheim"      decided d-0006
place helheim      "Helheim"       decided d-0006

adjacent(asgard, midgard)          decided d-0004
adjacent(asgard, vanaheim)         decided d-0004
adjacent(midgard, jotunheim)       decided d-0005
adjacent(midgard, svartalfheim)    decided d-0005
adjacent(vanaheim, alfheim)        decided d-0005
adjacent(muspelheim, niflheim)     decided d-0006
adjacent(niflheim, helheim)        decided d-0006
adjacent(jotunheim, niflheim)      decided d-0006
adjacent(asgard, alfheim)          proposed

# --- people, a thing, and facts -----------------------------------------------
person odin     "Odin All-Father"   decided d-0006
person thor     "Thor"              decided d-0007
person loki     "Loki"              decided d-0007
person heimdall "Heimdall"          decided d-0007
thing gungnir   "Gungnir"           decided d-0008
thing sol       "Sol, the Sun"      decided d-0008

fact odin     rules   asgard        decided d-0006
fact thor     guards  midgard       decided d-0007
fact heimdall watches asgard        decided d-0007

# --- states: facts acquire validity spans (the two-era sun case) --------------
state sol.course = crossing until the-last-battle   decided d-0008
state sol.course = devoured since the-last-battle   decided d-0008
# crossing UNTIL the wolf takes the sun at the last battle and devoured SINCE it:
# an elder-age flashback checks clean AND the wolf-age doom checks clean, because
# the two claims hold over disjoint spans (03-time §2). No false contradiction.

# --- a world rule with NEGATION: a certain-at-risk conclusion (01-syntax §3) ---
# A realm's ward holds where its wall is raised and it has not been stormed. The
# ground is certain; the storming is a mere shadow — proposed, not settled — so it
# does NOT block the derivation, but it POISONS: `ward-holds(asgard)` is derived
# certain (its support rests on the ratified `warded`) AND flagged certain-at-risk,
# defeasible should `stormed` ever be ruled true (02-semantics §4). Had `stormed`
# been certain, the certain-layer test would have blocked the rule outright.
warded(asgard)    ratified d-0011    # certain: the wall of Asgard stands
stormed(asgard)   proposed           # a shadow: possible at the doom, not certain

rule ward-holds   ratified d-0011
  ward-holds($r) :- warded($r), not stormed($r)

# --- a coined in-world term (05 §4) -------------------------------------------
# A `term` names one of the world's own ideas once, so a later claim can cite the
# term rather than re-gloss it. The doom is such an idea: named here (citing its
# own decision), reusable everywhere after.
term the-doom "Ragnarok, the fated fall of the gods and the burning of the Nine Realms"   decided d-0012

# --- the lexicon: a cited lexical ban (05 §4) ---------------------------------
# The register is kept saga-true by a ruling, so an anachronism is a cited lexicon
# fact, not a config entry. No name or diegetic term may carry it.
banned gunpowder d-0013 "the Nine Realms keep to seax and spear — no black-powder anachronism"
banned clockwork d-0013 "fate is spun by the Norns, never geared — no mechanism in-world"

# --- an open fork: a bare set of live options (01-syntax §4) -------------------
fork what-does-wisdom-cost
  option an-eye-at-the-well
  option a-nine-day-hanging

# --- an open question: options under a hypothesis (01-syntax §4) ---------------
hypothesis h-return "the world rises again, green, out of the sea after the fire"

question who-holds-the-reborn-world "Who holds the world after the fire?"
  option the-sons-of-thor implies holds(the-sons-of-thor, after-ragnarok)
  option baldr-returned   implies holds(baldr, after-ragnarok)   assumes h-return
# proposed-first: neither option is canon until a ruling; the question is a
# tracked debt, not a blank. `baldr-returned` presumes the world is reborn, so it
# rides the h-return hypothesis; `the-sons-of-thor` stands on its own.

# --- a settled hypothesis: the ratify BLOCK that turns a supposition certain ---
# The counterpart to h-return's live question: a hypothesis the author HAS ruled
# on. A bare `ratify d-000n` (the ledger above) mints a decision; a ratify BLOCK
# additionally settles a named hypothesis — the claim that `assumes` it flips from
# merely-possible to certain in one recorded act (05 §5). This block also declares
# d-0014, so its citation carries authority like any ledger entry.
hypothesis h-seer-foresaw "the seeress foresaw the doom from the first, and sang it before it came"
event the-first-prophecy   during the-binding-age   proposed assumes h-seer-foresaw
ratify d-0014 { h-seer-foresaw := true }
